# Core Principles

- TypeScript strict mode, readonly types by default
- Functional programming over classes
- Immutable data patterns
- Explicit error handling

# Code Style

- Use the `function` keyword for pure functions
- Avoid unnecessary curly braces in conditionals; use concise syntax for simple statements
- Always use braces around switch case statements
- Avoid using let unless it adds substantial clarity
- Use the nullish coalescing operator (`??`) instead of a logical or (`||`) when possible.
- Do not mutate data unless absolutely necessary
- Do not directly pass functions to map, always use an intermediate variable, ex: `array.map((element) => transform(element))` instead of `array.map(transform)`
- Use the syntax `type Foo = Readonly<{ ... }>` rather than marking exact member(s) of the type as `readonly`
- Avoid `null`, always prefer `undefined`

# Type Safety

- Strict types, avoid `any`
- Use `as const` for literal types
- Prefer type predicates over type assertions
- Use discriminated unions for state management
- Export types from their defining modules
- Use `type` imports for type-only imports

# Code Organization

- Group imports: external deps, internal modules, relative imports
- Use barrel exports (`index.ts`) for modules
- Prefer named exports over default exports
- One concern per file, clear single responsibility

# Naming Conventions

- Use PascalCase for types, interfaces, classes
- Use camelCase for functions, variables, properties
- Use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for constants
- Use descriptive names that reveal intent

# Error Handling

- Use Result/Either patterns for fallible operations
- Throw errors early, handle at boundaries
- Prefer explicit error types over generic Error

# Routing

Use TanStack Start with TanStack Router's file-based routing. Routes use `createFileRoute` to define the route and parameters. No need to update other files when adding new routes.

Related routes should be grouped together using TanStack Router route groups. Use named groups if they share a URL prefix, use unnamed groups (beginning with underscore) if they are related but do not share a URL prefix.

For example, routes under the URL `/dashboard` would be organized as follows:

- `src/routes/dashboard`
  - `src/routes/dashboard/index.tsx` - `/dashboard`
  - `src/routes/dashboard/reports.tsx` - `/dashboard/reports`

and marketing routes which are logically similar, may share a layout, etc. but have disparate URLs would be organized as follows:

- `src/routes/_marketing`
  - `src/routes/_marketing/promos.tsx` - `/promos`
  - `src/routes/_marketing/referral.tsx` - `/referral`

# Components

- Use React with functional components.
- Do not use `React.FC`, just use plain functions with properties defined by a named type, for example, if the component is called `NavigationMenu` the props type should be called `NavigationMenuProps`.
- Use `PropsWithChildren` as the prop type for any component that takes `children` (extend with additional properties if the component needs it)
- Prefer named exports for components
- Export all react prop types from their components
- Do not create large components. Create well named components with a clear single responsibility. For example, if a navigation component has a list of links in it, create a `Navigation` component that composes a `NavigationList` with `NavigationListItem` components

# Import Organization

- External dependencies first
- `@/` aliases for internal modules
- Relative imports last
- Group React imports: `import { type ComponentProps } from 'react'`

# State Management

- Use `useState` for local component state
- Use `useReducer` for complex state logic
- Use TanStack Query for server state
- Avoid prop drilling beyond 3 levels - use context

# Backend, data loading, actions

Use TanStack Start's data loader to perform server side data loading for all initial loading. For example:

```typescript
export const Route = createFileRoute("/_authed/dashboard")({
  component: Dashboard,
  loader: async ({ context }) => {
    const db = connectToDb(process.env.DATABASE_URL!);
    const posts = await Posts.getAll(db);

    return { posts };
  },
});

function Dashboard() {
  const { posts } = Route.useLoaderData();
}
```

Use TanStack Start server functions for all backend functionality (form handling, data mutation, API calls, etc.). Use Zod for any data validation.

For example:

```typescript
const ContactFormDataSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string(),
  email: z.string().email(),
  message: z.string(),
});

export const handleContactFormSubmit = createServerFn({
  method: "POST",
})
  .validator(ContactFormDataSchema)
  .handler(async ({ data: { name, email, message } }) => {
    try {
      await ContactService.submitForm(data);
      return { success: true };
    } catch (error) {
      throw new Error("Failed to submit form");
    }
  });

function SampleForm() {
  const form = useForm({
    defaultValues: {
      name: "",
      email: "",
      message: "",
    },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {
      const response = await handleContactFormSubmit({ data: value });
    },
  });
}
```

If complex compound operations are required such as calling an API, then updating a database record, then performing another API call they should be defined in `src/services` with the server function calling a function exported by a module there.

For example, if we have a sign up process that requires the following:

1. Create or update the user in our database.
2. Call the Resend email API to create a contact
3. Track in PostHog analytics that a new user has been created

We would create the following a new service in `src/services/registration.ts` with the following function:

```typescript
export async function signUp(email: string, clerkId: string) {
  await Users.createOrUpdate({ email, clerkId });
  await Resend.createContact(email);
  await PostHogClient.track("user.created", { email });
}
```

Then in our TanStack Server function we could call `signUp` to perform the operation. This ensures our server functions have clear flow with the underlying complexity relegated to well defined services and enables us to easily add testing.

# Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS v4 for styling
  - Use shorthand classes like `size` when they are appropriate, for example: `w-6 h-6` should be `size-6`
  - Do not use absolute values for sizes and colors unless there is no close equivalent in Tailwind (for example, you need much larger sizes or a very specific brand color)
- Use class-variance-authority to create components that have variants and overridable classes
- Use the `cn` utility from `lib/utils/tailwind.css` to compose classes (this uses `clsx` and tailwind merge to ensure they are combined properly)

# Forms

Use Tanstack Form v1 for all forms with `createFormHook` to define commonly used form fields in a single component and a component per form that uses our `useAppForm` along with the `form.AppField` pattern. This ensures all forms have the same look and feel and we can update the form styling in one place.

For example, we should have a component `form.tsx` which provides our app from with common fields:

```typescript
import {
  AnyFieldApi,
  createFormHook,
  createFormHookContexts,
} from "@tanstack/react-form";

const { fieldContext, formContext, useFieldContext } = createFormHookContexts();

type TextFieldProps = Readonly<{
  label: string;
  disabled?: boolean;
}>;

function TextField({ label, disabled }: TextFieldProps) {
  const field = useFieldContext<string>();

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor={field.name}>{label}</label>
      <input
        id={field.name}
        name={field.name}
        value={field.state.value}
        onChange={(event) => field.handleChange(event.target.value)}
        onBlur={field.handleBlur}
        disabled={disabled}
      />
      <FormMessage field={field} />
    </div>
  );
}

export const { useAppForm } = createFormHook({
  fieldComponents: {
    TextField,
  },
  formComponents: {},
  fieldContext,
  formContext,
});
```

Using our form component this is what our forms should look like:

```typescript
import { useAppForm } from "@/components/form";

const formSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(2, "Name must be at least 2 characters"),
  email: z.string().email("Invalid email address"),
});

export type SignupFormValues = z.infer<typeof formSchema>;

type SignupFormProps = Readonly<{
  onSubmit: (data: SignupFormValues) => void;
  initialValues?: SignupFormValues;
}>;

export function SignupForm({ onSubmit, initialValues }: SignupFormProps) {
  const form = useAppForm({
    defaultValues: initialValues ?? {
      name: "",
      email: "",
    },
    validators: {
      onChange: formSchema,
    },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {
      await onSubmit(value);
    },
  });

  return (
    <form
      onSubmit={(event) => {
        event.preventDefault();
        event.stopPropagation();
        void form.handleSubmit();
      }}
      className="space-y-4"
    >
      <form.AppField name="name">
        {(field) => <field.TextField label="Name" />}
      </form.AppField>

      <form.AppField name="email">
        {(field) => <field.TextField label="Email" />}
      </form.AppField>

      <button type="submit" disabled={!form.state.canSubmit}>
        Submit
      </button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

# Authentication

We use [Better Auth](https://www.better-auth.com/) for authentication.

The route group `_authed` is preconfigured so that it enforces authentication for all routes nested underneath it. Create any routes that require authentication under this route group.

If access to the user object is needed, it is available in the router context and may be accessed as follows:

```typescript
function Dashboard() {
  const { authSession } = Route.useRouteContext();

  // authSession.user is the Better Auth user object
  return <div>Welcome {authSession.user.email}</div>;
}
```

# File Organization

- Components in `src/components/`
- Business logic in `src/services/`
- Types in same file as implementation
- Shared types in `src/types/`

# Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements first
- Include ARIA labels for interactive elements
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Test with screen readers

# Loading & Error States

- Use Suspense for data loading
- Implement error boundaries at route level
- Show loading skeletons, not spinners

# Performance

- Use React.memo sparingly, only for expensive renders
- Prefer server-side data fetching
- Use TanStack Query for client-side caching when needed
